    {
      "source": "Doctors of Running",
      "priority": 3,
      "url": "https://www.doctorsofrunning.com/2025/12/asics-metaspeed-ray-review-2025.html",
      "type": "qualitative",
      "keyFindings": [
        "Cushioning: FF Leap is described as extremely soft/compliant and very protective for such a light racer, with excellent cushioning for long distances; heel feel is plush via a large heel bevel, and the forefoot feels smooth with an early rocker and slight plate flexibility.",
        "Responsiveness: Energy return is a standout, with a very bouncy, poppy rebound that feels nearly unmatched in this tester’s experience; the shoe feels quick to roll and snappy at faster paces.",
        "Stability: Even with a plated, relatively rigid platform and secure upper lockdown, the shallow heel counter, very soft foam, and narrow midfoot create quick pronation and reduced lateral stability; normal turns are manageable, but sharp turns can feel squirrely.",
        "Durability: Expected durability is normal to slightly below normal; visible midsole creasing occurs from compression, and the review does not report exceptional outsole wear resistance."
      ]
    },
    {
      "source": "Road Trail Run",
      "priority": 4,
      "url": "https://www.roadtrailrun.com/2025/11/asics-metaspeed-ray-review.html",
      "type": "qualitative",
      "keyFindings": [
        "Cushioning: FF Leap is exceptionally soft with deep sink-in and strong impact protection, but heel landings feel less supported while forefoot landings feel plush and protected.",
        "Responsiveness: The shoe has sharp rebound and trampoline-like bounce with snappy turnover, especially at tempo and marathon pace when the forefoot plate is consistently loaded.",
        "Stability: Stability is the main weakness; very soft tall foam, a narrow heel and midfoot, minimal upper control, and a soft collapsible heel counter produce high lateral wobble and low confidence on turns or uneven surfaces.",
        "Durability: Outsole coverage is minimal and wet-corner traction is only moderate; expected longevity is good but limited (around 200 miles rather than 400), with no major wear issues observed after 50 miles."
      ]
    },
    {
      "source": "Believe in the Run",
      "priority": 5,
      "url": "https://believeintherun.com/shoe-reviews/asics-metaspeed-ray-review/",
      "type": "qualitative",
      "keyFindings": [
        "Cushioning: Full-length FF Leap foam and a high stack (39.5 mm heel / 34.5 mm forefoot) create a very soft, plush, sink-in ride with strong impact protection, but limited structure as fatigue sets in.",
        "Responsiveness: The shoe is highly energetic and race-ready, with clear bounce, pop, and propulsive energy return that keeps turnover feeling lively at faster paces.",
        "Stability: Stability is a clear weakness; the soft platform feels squirrelly/wobbly (especially at easy pace or on uneven ground), with only partial help from the wider base for lateral control.",
        "Durability: Longevity appears poor for most runners, with visible heavy wear by about 52 miles and comments suggesting more of a one- to two-race shoe, despite strong wet-condition grip from Asicsgrip."
      ]
    }
  ],
